 Tong Han and Huiguang He and Ce Chen and Qihong Zou and Hesheng Liu and Hui Zhang and Chao Chai and Chunming Lu and Yiheng Tu and Yong Liu and Danhua Lin and Weihua Zhao and Xiufeng Xu and Xiaoli Liu and Zaixu Cui and Zheng Wang and Ruiwang Huang and Zhanjiang Li and Yunzhe Liu and Xiaojun Li and Xiujie Yang and Nan Zhang and Antao Chen and Bin Zhang and Pengmin Qin and Chen Liu and Zhenwei Yao and Yanjun Wei and Huishu Yuan and Feng Wang and Yu Zhang and Quan Zhang and Fang Hu and Huan Xie and Xuehai Wu and Jiaojian Wang and Guo-Guang Fan and Zhiqun Wang and Dongling Zhang and Hui Zhong and Yonggang Wang and Lijun Bai and Yongmei Li and Xinhua Wei and Jinhui Wang and Yi Zhang and Hongjian He and Shuyu Li and Tijiang Zhang and Fan Jiang and Jian Yang and Feiyan Chen and Alzheimer’s Disease Neuroimaging Initiative - and CHIMGEN Consortium and DIDA-MDD Working Group - and MCADI Consortium - and Chinese Lifespan Brain Mapping Consortium - and Tian Tian and Long Jiang Zhang and Dongtao Wei and Jiajia Zhu and Ching-Po Lin and Feng Feng and Kuncheng Li and Xiaochu Zhang and Zhaoxiang Ye and Qi Dong and Wen Qin and Yong He},
  year = {2026},
  doi = {10.21203/rs.3.rs-10588519/v1},
  url = {https://doi.org/10.21203/rs.3.rs-10588519/v1},
  howpublished = {Research Square},
  annotation = {中文辅助译名：中国和西方人群脑不对称的差异寿命轨迹},
  abstract = {Abstract Cerebral asymmetry is a core principle of human brain organization, showing dynamic changes across the lifespan and alterations in brain disorders. However, it remains unclear whether lifespan trajectories of asymmetry differ across populations. We compared lifespan structural asymmetry normative charts of 221 cerebral imaging phenotypes from 43,037 Chinese and 56,339 Western participants aged 0–100 years. The two populations showed distinct lifespan asymmetry patterns in 26.2% of the phenotypes. Chinese-minus-Western asymmetry difference curves displayed distinct patterns across brain phenotypes: rightward (45.7%), leftward (26.2%), rightward-to-leftward (11.8%), leftward-to-rightward (10.0%), and unclassified (6.3%). Population-matched normative models outperformed population-unmatched normative models in capturing normal asymmetry variability among healthy individuals and in detecting abnormal asymmetry deviations in patients with Alzheimer’s disease, mild cognitive impairment, schizophrenia, and major depressive disorder. These findings indicate that population mismatch can bias chart-based individual-level asymmetry assessment and underscore the need for population-representative brain asymmetry normative charts.},
}

