  year = {2018},
  doi = {10.1016/j.jalz.2018.06.2096},
  url = {https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jalz.2018.06.2096},
  howpublished = {Alzheimer s & Dementia},
  annotation = {中文辅助译名：IC-P-032：网络构建方法对阿尔茨海默病路径长度值的影响：MRI 连接性研究的多项研究分析},
  abstract = {Brain connectivity is disrupted in Alzheimer's disease (AD). However, diverging results have been reported for the directionality of network measure differences between patients and controls (CN), especially for path length values. Here, we investigated the influence of network construction choices on path length differences between controls and AD subjects in an ongoing subject-level multi-study analysis of MRI connectivity studies on AD. We collected data from 35 studies (sMRI, DTI, fMRI, PET, MEG/EEG). Here, we present data for 8 studies for which preprocessing so far has been completed (2 sMRI, 6 rs-fMRI; see Fig.1 for study details), including 355 CN (mean age 73.6±6.8 years, 55.8% female) and 383 AD patients (mean age 73.4±7.7 years, 50.4 % female). We tested the influence of two types of thresholds on path length values: a commonly used connectivity density of 15% and the minimum connectivity density to keep the network fully connected. For each modality and type of threshold, we tested differences in path length values between CN and AD with linear mixed models including study where the data originated as random effect. Overview of included studies and network analysis. The path length was calculated after binarization of the respective network. Abbreviations: sMRI, structural MRI; rs-fMRI, resting state functional MRI. When networks had the same connectivity density of 15%, AD subjects tended to show higher path length values in rs-fMRI (β±SE: 0.19±0.11; p=0.07; Fig.},
}

